1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:05,471
For a coronary angiogram, the team may go
through your wrist or your groin.

2
00:00:06,075 --> 00:00:09,314
Both routes reach the arteries that supply
your heart.

3
00:00:09,918 --> 00:00:12,275
The choice can affect your recovery.

4
00:00:12,786 --> 00:00:16,780
Wrist access enters the radial artery on
the thumb side.

5
00:00:17,384 --> 00:00:21,923
Groin access enters the common femoral
artery at the top of the thigh.

6
00:00:22,527 --> 00:00:27,763
The team numbs the skin and guides a thin
tube called a catheter through the arteries

7
00:00:27,821 --> 00:00:28,668
to the heart.

8
00:00:29,481 --> 00:00:33,719
An angiogram uses contrast and X-rays to
show narrowings.

9
00:00:34,416 --> 00:00:39,617
Percutaneous coronary intervention, or PCI,
is a catheter-based

10
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:44,400
treatment. The team may use a balloon or a
stent to treat a narrowing.

11
00:00:45,097 --> 00:00:48,069
Either route can be used for the test or
treatment.

12
00:00:48,673 --> 00:00:52,643
Wrist access is common for a planned
angiogram when suitable.

13
00:00:53,247 --> 00:00:58,681
For PCI during a heart attack or another
acute coronary syndrome, guidelines prefer

14
00:00:58,739 --> 00:01:03,678
the wrist when feasible. In that group it
lowers the risk of bleeding and blood-

15
00:01:03,678 --> 00:01:09,002
vessel complications. The US guideline also
reports a lower risk of death.

16
00:01:09,443 --> 00:01:14,818
For PCI in stable coronary disease, wrist
access helps reduce bleeding and vessel

17
00:01:14,876 --> 00:01:16,432
problems at the entry site.

18
00:01:16,873 --> 00:01:21,285
The groin artery can fit larger equipment,
including some devices that help the heart

19
00:01:21,366 --> 00:01:26,951
pump in emergencies. The team may also use
it if the wrist artery is small or

20
00:01:27,032 --> 00:01:32,581
hard to pass through. Previous bypass
surgery or plans to use a wrist artery

21
00:01:32,639 --> 00:01:34,845
for bypass can affect the choice.

22
00:01:35,449 --> 00:01:40,035
Sometimes a wrist artery tightens around
the catheter or the path is difficult to

23
00:01:40,105 --> 00:01:44,900
navigate. The team may switch to the other
wrist or to the groin.

24
00:01:45,410 --> 00:01:50,321
If two sites were used, staff check both
and explain how to look after them.

25
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:53,933
At the wrist, staff usually use a pressure
band.

26
00:01:54,142 --> 00:01:57,845
They gradually ease the pressure while
checking the site and your hand.

27
00:01:58,356 --> 00:02:03,325
At the groin, they may press by hand or use
a closure device, such as a plug or

28
00:02:03,395 --> 00:02:06,680
stitch. They check for bleeding with either
route.

29
00:02:07,191 --> 00:02:10,337
After wrist access you can often sit up
sooner.

30
00:02:10,511 --> 00:02:14,424
After groin access you may need to lie flat
with your leg straight.

31
00:02:14,702 --> 00:02:19,265
When you can walk depends on how the site
was closed, the medicines you received and

32
00:02:19,335 --> 00:02:23,363
how you’re doing. Wait for staff to tell
you it’s safe.

33
00:02:23,572 --> 00:02:28,286
Some people go home the same day after a
straightforward planned angiogram.

34
00:02:28,889 --> 00:02:33,731
After a heart attack or if you need more
monitoring, you may stay longer.

35
00:02:34,845 --> 00:02:39,814
Ask for written instructions on your
dressing, medicines, lifting, driving and

36
00:02:39,895 --> 00:02:42,775
work, and whom to call with concerns.

37
00:02:43,471 --> 00:02:47,442
A small bruise and mild soreness at either
site can happen.

38
00:02:47,953 --> 00:02:53,340
Contact your care team promptly if pain,
redness or swelling increases,

39
00:02:53,514 --> 00:02:57,334
if fluid drains from the site, or if you
develop a fever.

40
00:02:57,844 --> 00:03:00,724
If the site starts bleeding, apply firm
pressure.

41
00:03:00,933 --> 00:03:05,043
Call your local emergency number for heavy
bleeding, bleeding that won’t stop with

42
00:03:05,101 --> 00:03:07,841
pressure, or a swelling that grows quickly.

43
00:03:08,653 --> 00:03:13,971
Get emergency help if the hand or foot on
that side suddenly becomes cold, pale or

44
00:03:14,052 --> 00:03:19,508
numb, or if you have severe chest pain or
sudden trouble breathing.
